WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.139 --> 00:00:08.210
Elif - welcome, it's so good to see you here
in London.

00:00:08.210 --> 00:00:11.639
In a couple of months we'll meet again in
Amsterdam, as you are one of the speakers

00:00:11.639 --> 00:00:14.900
for our conference 'What Will Save the World'.

00:00:14.900 --> 00:00:19.429
Would you please be so kind to introduce yourself
to the Dutch audience?

00:00:19.429 --> 00:00:25.779
You're a cosmopolitan, you're a novelist;
maybe you can tell us a little bit about yourself.

00:00:25.779 --> 00:00:26.820
With pleasure.

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:31.909
I'm a writer, a novelist, a storyteller.

00:00:31.909 --> 00:00:37.559
That's how I connect with the world, through
stories and through imagination.

00:00:37.559 --> 00:00:42.449
That said, I also have an academic background,
an interdisciplinary background.

00:00:42.449 --> 00:00:49.539
I was educated in international relations,
then I have done a Master's degree in women's

00:00:49.539 --> 00:00:54.180
studies and then a PhD in political science.

00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:59.590
I have always kept my connection with academia
throughout the years.

00:00:59.590 --> 00:01:06.369
I'm also a public speaker and commentator
on various issues that I think are important

00:01:06.369 --> 00:01:11.930
in the world today, so I do write for various
publications around the world.

00:01:11.930 --> 00:01:17.230
To me, all of that is in harmony, but at the
same time there may be a gap,

00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:19.640
because by nature I am an introvert.

00:01:19.650 --> 00:01:22.900
As a writer, my job is based on solitude.

00:01:22.900 --> 00:01:29.190
But I also have this other side that likes
to engage in public debates and likes to think

00:01:29.190 --> 00:01:31.950
about the issues that matter today.

00:01:31.950 --> 00:01:39.760
One of the gifts of being a writer is the
power of imagination, and you have a huge

00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:44.640
power of imagination, as everybody who has
read your books knows.

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:52.930
But when you look at today's world as a writer,
then what is it that you see?

00:01:52.930 --> 00:01:57.210
I see lots of signs that I find quite alarming.

00:01:57.210 --> 00:02:03.780
Maybe I am a Gramscian in that regard, I always
share this pessimism of the intellect and

00:02:03.780 --> 00:02:06.259
the optimism of the will, of the heart.

00:02:06.259 --> 00:02:09.920
So I'm half pessimistic, half optimistic.

00:02:09.920 --> 00:02:16.230
I am half pessimistic because, when I look
at the signs today, I see a very strong trend

00:02:16.230 --> 00:02:23.730
back to what I call tribalism, back to nation
states, nationalism, religiosity - all those

00:02:23.730 --> 00:02:32.099
divisive forces that many intellectuals in
the 1940s and '50s thought were going to disappear,

00:02:32.099 --> 00:02:33.099
gradually.

00:02:33.099 --> 00:02:34.099
That did not happen.

00:02:34.099 --> 00:02:37.980
In fact the opposite happened; they made a
very strong comeback.

00:02:37.980 --> 00:02:45.709
Religion is a major factor today, nationalism
indeed so, but also this tribalism that is

00:02:45.709 --> 00:02:49.660
dividing humanity on the basis of imaginary
categories.

00:02:49.660 --> 00:02:57.000
I am half optimistic when I look at the people,
global souls, I am a big believer in cosmopolitanism.

00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:02.510
I'm a big believer in the truth that, in this
world, if we're ever going to learn anything,

00:03:02.510 --> 00:03:06.300
we will learn it from people who are different
from us.

00:03:06.300 --> 00:03:11.060
I have nothing to learn from someone who is
exactly like me, who thinks exactly like me,

00:03:11.060 --> 00:03:15.410
but someone who is different will challenge
me, and perhaps I'll challenge him too.

00:03:15.410 --> 00:03:22.830
So that diversity, that cosmopolitanism that
we are belittling today is something that

00:03:22.830 --> 00:03:24.730
we need to renew.

00:03:24.730 --> 00:03:30.910
I also believe in the power of humanism, in
the role of empathy on which the art of storytelling

00:03:30.910 --> 00:03:32.480
is very much based.

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:41.200
But then the question is, and we will of course
discuss it at length at the conference:

00:03:41.360 --> 00:03:44.980
the big question is, what happened?

00:03:44.989 --> 00:03:53.620
After World War 2 we built our international
institutions like the United Nations.

00:03:53.620 --> 00:04:05.610
We have wonderful NGOs like Human Rights Watch,
Amnesty International.

00:04:05.610 --> 00:04:11.920
We became more prosperous, there's a huge
development in technology and science.

00:04:11.920 --> 00:04:18.580
Then the fall of the Berlin wall; there was
a huge sense of optimism in the early '90s.

00:04:18.580 --> 00:04:26.890
So how come, do you think, that we now have
this backward tendency?

00:04:26.890 --> 00:04:36.250
There was indeed a huge tide of optimism in
the sense that many smart intellectuals thought

00:04:36.250 --> 00:04:42.050
that, thanks to globalization, we were all
going to become a big global village, and

00:04:42.050 --> 00:04:49.380
everybody would have primarily economic and
social ties, and that would help transcend

00:04:49.380 --> 00:04:52.930
national and nationalistic boundaries.

00:04:52.930 --> 00:04:54.720
It did not happen in that way.

00:04:54.720 --> 00:05:00.480
I think we need to bear in mind that, when
we think about time, we tend to think that

00:05:00.480 --> 00:05:04.210
time always goes in a linear direction.

00:05:04.210 --> 00:05:09.280
But we have seen that time does not always
go forward: sometimes it draws circles,

00:05:09.280 --> 00:05:11.310
it can move backwards.

00:05:11.310 --> 00:05:14.940
In other words, what I'm trying to say is
that if we learn nothing from the mistakes

00:05:14.940 --> 00:05:20.310
of the past, we are bound to make those mistakes
again and again.

00:05:20.310 --> 00:05:23.180
That is what is so sad, how memory is lost.

00:05:23.180 --> 00:05:28.690
And I'm not talking about the very long-term
memory of centuries, I'm talking about recent

00:05:28.690 --> 00:05:30.060
political memory.

00:05:30.060 --> 00:05:36.380
Even with regards to Europe, the EU: how it
was established, how the journey began.

00:05:36.380 --> 00:05:43.090
Very few people today are talking about the
beginning, that spirit that initiated everything.

00:05:43.090 --> 00:05:47.440
So we tend to take things for granted very
easily.

00:05:47.440 --> 00:05:53.260
And I find myself in this a bit ironic situation,
to tell you the truth, because, coming from

00:05:53.260 --> 00:06:02.000
Turkey, a country that's on the fringes of
Europe, and I live in the UK, I find myself

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:05.950
defending more passionately what I regard
as European values.

00:06:05.950 --> 00:06:10.920
For me, the EU is not primarily a financial
project or an economic project; it is primarily

00:06:10.920 --> 00:06:15.020
about values, which mean a lot to me.

00:06:15.020 --> 00:06:19.720
By those values I mean freedom of speech,
the separation of powers, rule of law,

00:06:19.740 --> 00:06:26.080
definitely women's rights, LGBT rights, minority rights
and many other human rights that all together

00:06:26.080 --> 00:06:27.530
make sense.

00:06:27.530 --> 00:06:32.260
So I do not take these values for granted,
because I do know that societies can lose

00:06:32.260 --> 00:06:33.260
them.

00:06:33.260 --> 00:06:38.100
Neither do I take cosmopolitanism for granted,
because I come from a country that has lost

00:06:38.100 --> 00:06:40.350
its cosmopolitan heritage.

00:06:40.350 --> 00:06:42.350
And I think that by losing that, Turkey lost
a lot.

